{"results":[{"url":"https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/ramaswamy-campaign-says-it-produced-ad-with-amy-actons-mother-and-will-spend-millions-to-promote-it","final_url":"https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/ramaswamy-campaign-says-it-produced-ad-with-amy-actons-mother-and-will-spend-millions-to-promote-it","title":"Ramaswamy campaign says it produced ad with Amy Acton’s mother and will spend millions to promote it","description":"Questions about the video will likely shadow Republican Vivek Ramaswamy, a biotech billionaire who previously ran for president but has never before held public office, in the final two weeks of the contest.","language":"en","text":"By — Julie Carr Smyth, Associated Press Julie Carr Smyth, Associated Press Leave your feedback Share Copy URL https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/ramaswamy-campaign-says-it-produced-ad-with-amy-actons-mother-and-will-spend-millions-to-promote-it Email Facebook Twitter LinkedIn Pinterest Tumblr Share on Facebook Share on Twitter Ramaswamy campaign says it produced ad with Amy Acton's mother and will spend millions to promote it Politics Oct 21, 2026 2:51 PM EST COLUMBUS, Ohio (AP) — Republican Vivek Ramaswamy's team confirmed late Tuesday that it produced a controversial advertisement featuring the estranged mother of Amy Acton, and the campaign said it will spend $4 million to promote it despite facing bipartisan rebuke for dredging up the painful childhood of his Democratic opponent in Ohio's governor's race. WATCH: Vance says he's 'disgusted' by Republicans 'whining' about Ramaswamy attack ad The decision exacerbates a deeply personal element in one of the country's most closely watched and expensive campaigns for state office. Questions about the video will likely shadow Ramaswamy, a biotech billionaire who previously ran for president but has never before held public office, in the final two weeks of the contest. Democrats said he was growing desperate at a moment when more than 676,000 people have already cast ballots in Ohio. Educate your inbox Subscribe to Here’s the Deal, our politics newsletter for analysis you won’t find anywhere else. Enter your email address Subscribe Form error message goes here. Thank you. Please check your inbox to confirm. The advertisement features Acton's birth mother, Donna Arthur, calling her \"a liar\" and insinuating she has mental health issues. Acton, Ohio's former public health director, has made no secret of facing homelessness and abuse as a child. Arthur lost custody of her children after their stepfather was indicted for raping Acton. The video's origins were initially murky when it started circulating online. It first surfaced in a post by an Ohio political blogger, then spread across social media. It did not include the \"paid for\" tag that is legally required to air on television so viewers can know who produced and paid for it. D.J. Brynes, who blogs as \"The Rooster,\" told CNN he got the video from a voter who viewed it as part of a poll and recorded it. READ MORE: Trump wants the midterms to be all about him. Based on Democrats' ads, they are happy to oblige Connie Luck, a spokesperson for Ramaswamy's campaign, said Acton's mother was not paid for the one-minute video, which she said will stream online and air on television. Acton spent Tuesday condemning Ramaswamy for the ad even before his campaign confirmed its provenance. \"I won't normalize somebody who has said that my childhood is just something I'm complaining about, who today just released an ad using my birth mother, who I was removed from at the age of 12 for severe abuse and neglect, and sexual abuse,\" she told Spectrum News, a cable news outlet in Ohio. She said Ramaswamy made similar personal attacks during his run for the Republican presidential nomination two years ago, calling it \"a lifelong pattern.\" READ MORE: After taxpayer-funded ads glorifying Trump sparked backlash, he says his super PAC will pay instead Ramaswamy's campaign initially issued a statement that did not address whether he was responsible for the ad or knew who produced it. In the earlier statement, Luck said Acton \"has some serious problems with the truth that the public needs to be aware of.\" Democrats and Republicans condemn the ad Casey Putsch, a car designer and YouTube provocateur who competed against Ramaswamy for the Republican nomination for governor, said on social media that he would be voting for Acton in the wake of the ad's release. He called the video \"pretty damn low.\" Acton's campaign released a statement from her brother, Philip Stearns, who said the two were removed from their mother Donna Arthur's custody as children after their stepfather, Arthur's husband, was indicted for raping Acton as a child. Acton has not seen Arthur, whose last known address was in Pennsylvania, since they were in a courtroom together in 1978. WATCH: Ohio becomes midterm battleground after years of Republican wins Later Tuesday, the Acton campaign released a video featuring Stearns, who recalls his older sister taking \"the worst\" of the abuse they faced as children in order to protect him and disparages Ramaswamy and his allies for exploiting the situation. According to police and court documents obtained by The Associated Press, Cledith Arthur was arrested and charged with the rape of his stepdaughter, then 12, in July 1978, after Acton's biological father reported him to authorities. The abuse allegedly happened from December 1976 to April 1978. Cledith Arthur pleaded not guilty. He was indicted by a grand jury that September, but failed to show at a court hearing and remained a fugitive until at least 1981. He has since died. High-profile Republicans who back Ramaswamy — including Donald Trump Jr. and influencer Laura Loomer — elevated the video in online posts. But others joined Democrats in condemning the ad. Meghan McCain called it \"dastardly\" and Dan Hauser, a strategist for Republican Sen. Mike Lee of Utah, said he deleted his post sharing the video once he realized \"the very serious accusations that caused their issues.\" The Democrat running for U.S. Senate in Ohio, Sherrod Brown, described the spot as \"disgusting\" and said everyone — including his opponent, Sen. Jon Husted — should be willing to condemn it. Husted's campaign didn't respond to a request for comment. Associated Press writer Ali Swenson in New York contributed to this report. A free press is a cornerstone of a healthy democracy. Support trusted journalism and civil dialogue. Donate now By — Julie Carr Smyth, Associated Press Julie Carr Smyth, Associated Press\n\nCOLUMBUS, Ohio (AP) — Republican Vivek Ramaswamy's team confirmed late Tuesday that it produced a controversial advertisement featuring the estranged mother of Amy Acton, and the campaign said it will spend $4 million to promote it despite facing bipartisan rebuke for dredging up the painful childhood of his Democratic opponent in Ohio's governor's race. WATCH: Vance says he's 'disgusted' by Republicans 'whining' about Ramaswamy attack ad The decision exacerbates a deeply personal element in one of the country's most closely watched and expensive campaigns for state office. Questions about the video will likely shadow Ramaswamy, a biotech billionaire who previously ran for president but has never before held public office, in the final two weeks of the contest. Democrats said he was growing desperate at a moment when more than 676,000 people have already cast ballots in Ohio. Educate your inbox Subscribe to Here’s the Deal, our politics newsletter for analysis you won’t find anywhere else. Enter your email address Subscribe Form error message goes here. Thank you. Please check your inbox to confirm. The advertisement features Acton's birth mother, Donna Arthur, calling her \"a liar\" and insinuating she has mental health issues. Acton, Ohio's former public health director, has made no secret of facing homelessness and abuse as a child. Arthur lost custody of her children after their stepfather was indicted for raping Acton. The video's origins were initially murky when it started circulating online. It first surfaced in a post by an Ohio political blogger, then spread across social media. It did not include the \"paid for\" tag that is legally required to air on television so viewers can know who produced and paid for it. D.J. Brynes, who blogs as \"The Rooster,\" told CNN he got the video from a voter who viewed it as part of a poll and recorded it. READ MORE: Trump wants the midterms to be all about him. Based on Democrats' ads, they are happy to oblige Connie Luck, a spokesperson for Ramaswamy's campaign, said Acton's mother was not paid for the one-minute video, which she said will stream online and air on television. Acton spent Tuesday condemning Ramaswamy for the ad even before his campaign confirmed its provenance. \"I won't normalize somebody who has said that my childhood is just something I'm complaining about, who today just released an ad using my birth mother, who I was removed from at the age of 12 for severe abuse and neglect, and sexual abuse,\" she told Spectrum News, a cable news outlet in Ohio. She said Ramaswamy made similar personal attacks during his run for the Republican presidential nomination two years ago, calling it \"a lifelong pattern.\" READ MORE: After taxpayer-funded ads glorifying Trump sparked backlash, he says his super PAC will pay instead Ramaswamy's campaign initially issued a statement that did not address whether he was responsible for the ad or knew who produced it. In the earlier statement, Luck said Acton \"has some serious problems with the truth that the public needs to be aware of.\" Democrats and Republicans condemn the ad Casey Putsch, a car designer and YouTube provocateur who competed against Ramaswamy for the Republican nomination for governor, said on social media that he would be voting for Acton in the wake of the ad's release. He called the video \"pretty damn low.\" Acton's campaign released a statement from her brother, Philip Stearns, who said the two were removed from their mother Donna Arthur's custody as children after their stepfather, Arthur's husband, was indicted for raping Acton as a child. Acton has not seen Arthur, whose last known address was in Pennsylvania, since they were in a courtroom together in 1978. WATCH: Ohio becomes midterm battleground after years of Republican wins Later Tuesday, the Acton campaign released a video featuring Stearns, who recalls his older sister taking \"the worst\" of the abuse they faced as children in order to protect him and disparages Ramaswamy and his allies for exploiting the situation. According to police and court documents obtained by The Associated Press, Cledith Arthur was arrested and charged with the rape of his stepdaughter, then 12, in July 1978, after Acton's biological father reported him to authorities. The abuse allegedly happened from December 1976 to April 1978. Cledith Arthur pleaded not guilty. He was indicted by a grand jury that September, but failed to show at a court hearing and remained a fugitive until at least 1981. He has since died. High-profile Republicans who back Ramaswamy — including Donald Trump Jr. and influencer Laura Loomer — elevated the video in online posts. But others joined Democrats in condemning the ad. Meghan McCain called it \"dastardly\" and Dan Hauser, a strategist for Republican Sen. Mike Lee of Utah, said he deleted his post sharing the video once he realized \"the very serious accusations that caused their issues.\" The Democrat running for U.S. Senate in Ohio, Sherrod Brown, described the spot as \"disgusting\" and said everyone — including his opponent, Sen. Jon Husted — should be willing to condemn it. Husted's campaign didn't respond to a request for comment. Associated Press writer Ali Swenson in New York contributed to this report. A free press is a cornerstone of a healthy democracy. Support trusted journalism and civil dialogue. Donate now","author":null,"published_date":"2026-10-21T14:51:45-05:00","latency_ms":1731.019313003344,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}